        "\n",
        "# Abstract {#section-1}\n",
        "\n",
        "Skip to the [data analysis](#section-4).\n",
        "\n",
        "The amount of industrial pollution released by European facilities each year is routinely measured and communicated in terms of tonnes of substances released. However, this metric provides an incomplete and potentially misleading picture, as it does not link industrial emissions to existing substance hazard classifications or provide further information on toxicity-weighted metrics. Despite various data sources being available, they have not yet been linked to provide a comprehensive overview. Currently, there is no Europe-wide database connecting industrial emissions to official substance hazard classifications and epidemiology-based characterisation factors (CFs) to assign meaning to and allow for interpreation of these raw mass releases. \n",
        "\n",
        "This research aims to address this gap by linking the European Pollutant Release and Transfer Register (E-PRTR) dataset to official substance hazard classifications and epidemiology-based characterisation factors (CFs). \n",
        "\n",
        "We enhanced the E-PRTR dataset by matching its reported pollutants to official substance hazard classifications using their official CAS and EC numbers. This included the European Registration, Evaluation, Authorisation and Restriction of Chemicals (REACH) system, its associated Classification, Labelling and Packaging of Chemicals (CLP) system and substances of very high concern (SVHC). We then linked the E-PRTR dataset to epidemiology-based characterisation factors (CFs) from the USEtox model. This enabled us to calculate human-toxicity-weighted metrics, and estimate the impact of the pollution on human health in terms of the number of Disability-Adjusted Life Years (DALYs). Finally, we overlay the E-PRTR dataset with the EU Census 2021 and geographical layers to analyse population count and distribution across Europe, in order to assess both the number of people, and their demographics, living near sources of hazardous industrial pollution, as well as potential geographical shifts in the location of hazardous industrial pollution over time.\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal of this study was to create a comprehensive database to improve understanding of the potential health impacts of industrial emissions in Europe, especially on their host populations, to enable better-informed decision-making and policy development.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "***\n",
        "# Hypothesis {#section-2}\n",
        "\n",
        "The burden of hosted industrial emissions of hazardous and carcinogenic substances is not evenly distributed across Europe, but is concentrated in a small number of places, with people living near these sites unwittingly residing in the immediate vicinity of installations that emit hazardous pollutants, and potentially suffering a disproportionately high number of years of life lost or years lived with disability.\n",
        "\n",
        "***\n",
        "# Research questions {#section-3}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Hosted source pollution pressure analysis:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Where are the facilities with the highest number of reported releases of hazardous and carcinogenic substances?\t\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Where are the facilities with the greatest potential impact on human health from these industrial releases?\t\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Has a shift in the location of hazardous industrial pollution occured over time between different regions of Europe?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Population-proximity analysis:\n",
        "\n",
        "4. How many people in Europe live near sources of hazardous industrial pollution?\t\n",
        "\n",
        "5. Which population groups live near these sources?\t\n",
        "\n",
        "## Health impact analysis:\n",
        "\n",
        "6. How many years of life do Europeans lose on average, or spend living with disabilities (DALYs), as a result of hazardous industrial pollution?\n",
        "\n",
        "7. Which European countries and regions have the highest DALY losses due to hazardous industrial pollution?\n",
        "\n",
        "***\n",
